{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 47, :comments ["<b>And the garment, when it has in it a plague of tzara'at</b> — This is not in nature at all, nor does it occur in the world; and the same is true of the plagues of houses. But when Israel is wholehearted toward HaShem, the spirit of HaShem rests upon them continually, to maintain their bodies, their garments, and their houses in good appearance. And when a sin or transgression occurs in one of them, a blemish appears in his flesh or in his garment or in his house, to show that HaShem has departed from upon him. Therefore Scripture says (Leviticus 14:34), \"And I will put a plague of tzara'at in a house of the land of your possession,\" for it is a blow from HaShem upon that house. And behold, it applies only in the land that is the inheritance of HaShem, as He said (ibid.), \"When you come into the land of Canaan, which I give to you for a possession.\" And the matter is not because it is an obligation of the land, but rather because that phenomenon will not occur except in the chosen land in which the glorious HaShem dwells in its midst.\n\nAnd in Torat Kohanim (Metzora, parasha 5:3) they further expounded that the house does not contract tumah except after conquest and division and when each and every one recognizes what is his. And the reason is that then their minds are settled upon them to know HaShem, and the Shekhinah dwells in their midst.\n\nAnd so too do I think regarding the plagues of garments, that they apply only in the land, and there was no need to restrict them outside the land, for they will never occur there. And because of this as well, they apply only to white garments, not to dyed ones, for perhaps the dye produced that blemish in that place according to its nature, and it is not the finger of Elohim. Therefore dyed garments contract tumah by the words of Rabbi Shimon (Negaim 11:3).\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, because of this, Scripture repeats in each and every verse, \"the garment or the skin or the warp or the woof,\" for the matter is a miracle.\n\nAnd our Sages have midrashim concerning them, all of them in Torat Kohanim:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {0 "The citation 'Torat Kohanim (Metzora, parasha 5:3)' refers to the Sifra on Leviticus 14:34; the specific parasha and verse numbering follows the standard edition of the Sifra."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:53:39.208590286Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}